<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Bourbon-Parma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Bourbon-Parma is a branch of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> founded by Philip, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-farnese">Elisabeth Farnese</a>, who became Duke of Parma in 1748 under the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle. The family ruled Parma until 1802, Lucca from 1815 to 1847 and Parma again until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> in 1859. Its descendants include the grand ducal family of Luxembourg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon-parma.jpg" alt="House of Bourbon-Parma" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katepanomegas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lesser_Arms_of_Bourbon-Parma.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After he became king of Naples in 1735, Parma passed to the Habsburgs, and the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 1748 then gave Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla to Philip of Bourbon, founding the <b>Bourbon-Parma</b> line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
